#: manual/design/immut.md:3
#: manual/design/internal.md:3
#: manual/design/mutable.md:3
#: manual/design/perf.md:3
#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:3
#: manual/design/perf_support.md:3
msgid "Design goal"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:5
msgid "`algebra` gives generic linear-algebra code a vocabulary for whole vector and matrix objects that is honest about what each object can do. A trait may only be implemented by a type that satisfies its laws, and an algorithm should be able to ask for exactly the structure it uses: a shape, an abelian group, a Hadamard ring, a transpose, or a matrix product. The package therefore defines small traits ordered by inclusion instead of one `Matrix` or `VectorSpace` trait. The [API page](../api/algebra.md) lists the traits; this page explains the mathematics they encode and the choices that follow from it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:14
#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:12
#: manual/design/backends/default.md:12
#: manual/design/consistency.md:12
#: manual/design/container.md:12
#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:11
#: manual/design/error.md:12
#: manual/design/immut.md:12
#: manual/design/internal.md:10
#: manual/design/mutable.md:14
#: manual/design/perf.md:9
#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:10
#: manual/design/perf_support.md:11
msgid "Mathematical background"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:16
msgid "Modules and vector spaces"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:18
msgid "Let $R$ be a ring with unit. A (left) $R$-module is an abelian group $(V, +, 0, -)$ together with a scalar action $R \\times V \\to V$, $(r, v) \\mapsto r v$, such that for all $r, s \\in R$ and $u, v \\in V$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:29
msgid "A vector space is a module over a field. Most of linear algebra is stated for vector spaces, but most of the *code* only needs much less: matrix addition needs the abelian group, matrix multiplication needs a semiring of scalars, and only elimination needs division. Integer matrices, for example, form a $\\mathbb{Z}$-module and are not a vector space; requiring a field would exclude them from algorithms that never divide."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:36
msgid "Coordinates"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:38
msgid "A dense vector of length $n$ over $R$ is an element of $R^n$, a dense $m \\times n$ matrix an element of $R^{m \\times n}$. Entry-wise addition makes both abelian groups; the scalar action $r \\cdot (a_{ij}) = (r a_{ij})$ makes them $R$-modules. For fixed $n$, the entry-wise product"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:47
msgid "makes $R^n$ a ring as well (the product ring), with unit $(1, \\dots, 1)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:49
msgid "Matrices as a category"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:51
msgid "Matrix multiplication is not an operation on one set. Its shape rule"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:57
msgid "says that matrices are the morphisms of a category $\\mathbf{Mat}_R$ whose objects are the natural numbers: an $m \\times n$ matrix is an arrow $n \\to m$, multiplication is composition, and the identity matrix $I_n$ is the identity arrow on $n$. A product $AB$ is defined exactly when the arrows are composable. Over a fixed object $n$ the arrows $n \\to n$ (the square matrices) form a ring, the familiar $M_n(R)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:64
msgid "Associativity of composition holds whenever $R$ is a semiring. For $A \\in R^{m \\times n}$, $B \\in R^{n \\times p}$, $C \\in R^{p \\times q}$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:82
msgid "Commutativity of multiplication was never used, so the law holds for matrices over any semiring, including non-commutative ones such as quaternions. The same computation with $C = I$ shows $AI = A$, and distributivity of the product over matrix addition follows entry-wise from distributivity in $R$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:87
msgid "Transpose"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:89
msgid "The transpose is the map $(A^{\\mathsf T})_{ij} = A_{ji}$ from $R^{m \\times n}$ to $R^{n \\times m}$. It is total and"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:97
msgid "The product rule needs more. For composable $A$ and $B$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:107
msgid "The two sums agree term by term exactly when $A_{kj} B_{ji} = B_{ji} A_{kj}$, that is, when the scalars commute. So $(AB)^{\\mathsf T} = B^{\\mathsf T} A^{\\mathsf T}$ is a theorem for matrices over a commutative ring and false in general.[^conj] Transpose is a contravariant functor $\\mathbf{Mat}_R \\to \\mathbf{Mat}_R$ only in the commutative case."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:113
msgid "For a ring with an involution $x \\mapsto \\bar{x}$ that reverses products, $\\overline{xy} = \\bar{y}\\,\\bar{x}$, the conjugate transpose $A^{*} = \\overline{A}^{\\mathsf T}$ does satisfy $(AB)^{*} = B^{*} A^{*}$ without commutativity, because each term becomes $\\overline{A_{kj} B_{ji}} = \\overline{B_{ji}}\\,\\overline{A_{kj}}$. This is why the concrete matrix types offer `adjoint` next to `transpose`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:119
#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:90
#: manual/design/backends/default.md:53
#: manual/design/consistency.md:44
#: manual/design/container.md:87
#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:37
#: manual/design/error.md:79
#: manual/design/immut.md:156
#: manual/design/internal.md:27
#: manual/design/mutable.md:270
#: manual/design/perf.md:21
#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:21
#: manual/design/perf_support.md:38
msgid "Design decisions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:121
msgid "Abelian group instead of module"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:123
msgid "**Problem.** The natural trait for \"a vector\" is an $R$-module, but a module has two carriers: the vectors and the scalars."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:126
msgid "**Options.** (a) A `Module` trait whose scalar type is fixed, for example `Double`. (b) A trait with an associated scalar type. (c) Only the additive structure, leaving scalars to concrete methods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:130
msgid "**Decision.** (c): `AdditiveVector` and `AdditiveMatrix` state the abelian group $(V, +, -)$ and nothing about scalars."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:133
msgid "**Why.** MoonBit traits have a single `Self` parameter and no associated types, so option (b) cannot be expressed. Option (a) would make every module over `Int`, `BigInt` or a user scalar type unrepresentable, and would hard-code one floating-point type into a public trait. Scalar multiplication therefore stays a method of the concrete types (`scale`, `left_scale`, `right_scale`) where the scalar type is known."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:140
msgid "No zero in the vector traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:142
msgid "A runtime-shaped vector type has no single zero: the identity of $(R^n, +)$ depends on $n$. A trait method `zero() -> Self` would have to choose a length. The traits therefore ask for `Add`, `Neg` and `Sub` only, and the group laws are stated without a named zero:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:151
msgid "The element $u + (-u)$ is the zero of the right length. The upstream `Zero` trait from `luna-generic` remains available for scalar types, where a single zero exists."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:155
msgid "Hadamard product as its own level"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:157
msgid "`Mul` on a vector type could mean the Hadamard product, a dot product, or a cross product, and only the first is closed ($R^n \\times R^n \\to R^n$ for all $n$). `VecMulVector` fixes the meaning to the Hadamard product and lives above `AdditiveVector`, so an algorithm that only adds vectors does not exclude types without a product. Scalar-valued products such as the dot product are maps $R^n \\times R^n \\to R$ out of the category of vectors; they are methods of the concrete backends (`DenseVector::dot`), not structure traits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:165
msgid "Matrix multiplication is partial and documented, not typed"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:167
msgid "**Problem.** On a runtime-shaped matrix type, `*` is defined only for composable shapes. A trait method returning `Self` cannot report failure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:170
msgid "**Options.** (a) Put `Mul` into the smallest matrix trait. (b) Encode shapes in types. (c) Separate shape, transpose and addition from multiplication, and let each implementation document the behaviour outside the domain."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:174
msgid "**Decision.** (c). `TransposeMatrix` and `AdditiveMatrix` are useful without multiplication, and `MatMulMatrix` is a separate level whose implementors must document their failure behaviour. A type with statically known shapes, such as a fixed $1 \\times 1$ or $3 \\times 3$ matrix, implements `MatMulMatrix` with a total `*`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:180
msgid "**Why.** MoonBit has no type-level naturals, so (b) is not available for dynamic dimensions. Option (a) would force a partial operation onto every matrix type, including ones that never multiply. Checked multiplication with a `Result` remains available on the concrete types (`@immut.Matrix::matmul`), where the error type is fixed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:186
msgid "Transpose returns `Self`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:188
msgid "`TransposeMatrix::transpose` returns the same type. Transpose is total on every shape, so it is a closed operation and fits a trait method. Whether the result is materialized or a view is not part of the contract; the [`container`](container.md) layer, by contrast, has a transpose that may produce a different target type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:194
#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:123
#: manual/design/backends/default.md:93
#: manual/design/consistency.md:59
#: manual/design/container.md:141
#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:76
#: manual/design/error.md:124
#: manual/design/immut.md:206
#: manual/design/internal.md:49
#: manual/design/mutable.md:350
#: manual/design/perf.md:38
#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:40
#: manual/design/perf_support.md:67
msgid "Correctness and invariants"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:196
msgid "The traits are empty or have one observation method, so correctness is a property of each implementation. The laws an implementation promises are:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:199
#: manual/integration/container.md:9
msgid "Level"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:199
msgid "Laws (whenever both sides are defined)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:201
msgid "abelian group laws; operations preserve shape"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:202
msgid "$R^n$ is a ring under `+` and `*`: associativity, distributivity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:203
msgid "$\\operatorname{shape}(A^{\\mathsf T}) = \\operatorname{shape}(A)$ reversed; $(A^{\\mathsf T})^{\\mathsf T} = A$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:205
msgid "$(AB)C = A(BC)$; distributivity; $(AB)^{\\mathsf T} = B^{\\mathsf T}A^{\\mathsf T}$ if the scalars commute"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:207
msgid "Two caveats apply to concrete scalar types."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:209
msgid "**Fixed-width integers.** `Int` is the ring $\\mathbb{Z}/2^{32}\\mathbb{Z}$, not $\\mathbb{Z}$. The laws above hold exactly in that ring, because wrapping addition and multiplication are the ring operations of $\\mathbb{Z}/2^{32}\\mathbb{Z}$; a matrix product that overflows is still the correct product modulo $2^{32}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:215
msgid "**Floating point.** `Float` and `Double` are not rings: addition is not associative under rounding. The laws hold only up to rounding error, and tests must compare with a tolerance. For an inner product of length $n$ computed in any order, the standard model $\\mathrm{fl}(x \\circ y) = (x \\circ y)(1 + \\delta)$, $|\\delta| \\le u$, gives"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:226
msgid "entry-wise, where $u = 2^{-53}$ for `Double`.[^higham] Applying the bound twice, each of $\\mathrm{fl}((AB)C)$ and $\\mathrm{fl}(A(BC))$ is within $2\\gamma_k\\,|A|\\,|B|\\,|C| + O(u^2)$ of the exact product, $k = \\max(n, p)$, so the two evaluation orders can differ by twice that amount. This is the scale a tolerance-based comparison must allow."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:232
msgid "N. J. Higham, *Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms*, 2nd ed., SIAM, 2002, §3.1 and §3.5. The bound follows by applying the model to each of the $n - 1$ additions and $n$ multiplications of one inner product."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:236
#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:135
#: manual/design/backends/default.md:104
#: manual/design/consistency.md:66
#: manual/design/container.md:156
#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:88
#: manual/design/error.md:140
#: manual/design/immut.md:224
#: manual/design/internal.md:56
#: manual/design/mutable.md:374
#: manual/design/perf.md:43
#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:46
#: manual/design/perf_support.md:74
msgid "Alternatives rejected"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:238
msgid "**A `VectorSpace` or `Module` trait.** Rejected until scalar association can be expressed without fixing a scalar type; see the first decision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:240
msgid "**A shape-dependent `zero(rows, cols)` method.** It would duplicate the upstream `Zero` trait with a different meaning and still could not serve generic code that does not know the shape."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:243
msgid "**Inner products and norms as structure traits.** They map into the scalars, depend on the scalar type (a norm into `Double` is not a norm into `Int`), and have several reasonable choices. They stay backend methods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:246
msgid "**One `Matrix` trait with every operation.** It would force partial multiplication and Hadamard products onto types that do not have them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:249
#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:145
#: manual/design/backends/default.md:114
#: manual/design/consistency.md:74
#: manual/design/container.md:168
#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:96
#: manual/design/error.md:149
#: manual/design/immut.md:234
#: manual/design/internal.md:62
#: manual/design/mutable.md:386
#: manual/design/perf.md:49
#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:51
#: manual/design/perf_support.md:80
msgid "Boundaries"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/algebra.md:251
msgid "`algebra` does not define scalar traits (those come from `luna-generic` and `arithmetic`), storage, element access, mutation or construction (those belong to [`container`](container.md)), checked error reporting, decompositions, solvers, norms or inner products. It does not provide implementations for the concrete `@immut` and `@mutable` types; [`backends/default`](backends/default.md) does that through owned wrapper types."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:1
msgid "arithmetic design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:5
msgid "Linear-algebra algorithms need a handful of scalar operations beyond the ring operators: an absolute value for pivoting, a square root for norms, a comparison for ordering, and a way to compare floating-point results. The `arithmetic` package names these operations for linear-algebra code without claiming that the scalar types satisfy laws they do not satisfy. It reuses the upstream vocabulary wherever it exists and adds a trait only where none does."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:14
msgid "Operations versus structures"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:16
msgid "A *structure* trait such as `Field` promises equations: associativity, distributivity, inverses. An *operation* trait such as `Sqrt` promises only that a function $\\sqrt{\\cdot} : T \\to T$ exists. The distinction matters because the builtin floating-point types implement the operations but satisfy the equations only approximately: for `Double`,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:27
msgid ""
"so $(a + b) - c \\n"
"e (a - c) + b$ in general. Every trait of this package is an operation trait."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:30
msgid "Partial operations and their totalizations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:32
msgid ""
"Division and square root are partial functions on the reals: $x / y$ is defined on $\\{(x, y) : y \\n"
"e 0\\}$ and $\\sqrt{x}$ on $\\{x \\ge 0\\}$. IEEE 754 makes them total by returning special values ($\\pm\\infty$, NaN), which then propagate silently. A checked operation instead totalizes a partial function $f : D \\rightharpoonup C$ into"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:47
msgid "where $E$ is a set of error values and $e(x)$ says *why* $x$ is outside the domain. `CheckedDiv` uses the following domain decomposition for floating-point operands:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:51
msgid "Region"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:51
msgid "IEEE result"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:51
msgid "Checked result"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:53
msgid ""
"$y \\n"
"e 0$, not both infinite"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:53
msgid "`Ok` of the same value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:54
#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:55
msgid "NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:55
msgid "$x, y$ both infinite"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:58
msgid "Ordering with NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:60
msgid "`Compare` on `Double` is not a total order: NaN is neither less than, equal to, nor greater than any value, so sorting or pivot selection on data containing NaN gives order-dependent results. `CheckedCompare` restricts the comparison to the ordered subset and reports `UnorderedComparison` outside it, which makes the result a total order on its domain."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:68
msgid "`ApproxEq` uses the absolute rule $a \\approx b \\iff |a - b| \\le \\varepsilon$. This relation is reflexive and symmetric but not transitive. From $|a - b| \\le \\varepsilon$ and $|b - c| \\le \\varepsilon$ the triangle inequality gives only"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:77
msgid ""
"and the bound is attained: with $a = 0$, $b = \\varepsilon$, $c = 2\\varepsilon$ we have $a \\approx b$, $b \\approx c$ and $a \\n"
"ot\\approx c$. So `approx_eq` is not an equivalence relation, and the package does not present it as one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:81
msgid "An absolute tolerance is also not scale-invariant. Floating-point spacing grows with magnitude: adjacent `Double` values near $x$ are about $2^{-52}|x|$ apart. Near $10^{20}$ that spacing is about $1.6 \\times 10^{4}$, so two different values can never be within $10^{-12}$; near $10^{-20}$ every pair of values is. A relative rule $|a - b| \\le \\varepsilon \\max(|a|, |b|)$ fixes the scale problem but fails near zero, which is why careful code combines both; this package leaves that choice to the caller."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:92
msgid "Reuse upstream names"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:94
msgid "The scalar traits `Zero`, `One`, `Inverse`, `Conjugate` and the analytic traits of `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` are re-exported with `pub using` rather than redefined. A local copy would create a second, incompatible `Sqrt`, and a scalar type implementing the upstream trait would not satisfy the local one. With `pub using`, `@la_arithmetic.Sqrt` *is* `@lf_arith.Sqrt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:100
msgid "Small local traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:102
msgid "`Abs`, `ApproxEq`, `CheckedDiv`, `CheckedSqrt` and `CheckedCompare` exist because linear-algebra code wanted these names when the upstream package did not provide them in this form. Each is one method, so a scalar type can opt in to exactly the operations it supports. The checked traits delegate to the upstream checked traits for `Float` and `Double`, so the two layers agree on every input."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:109
msgid "Context accepted and ignored for binary floating point"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:111
msgid "`checked_div` and `checked_sqrt` take an `ArithmeticContext` so that one signature serves fixed-precision and arbitrary-precision scalar types. For `Float` and `Double` the precision is fixed by the hardware format and rounding is round-to-nearest-even, so the context has no effect."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:116
msgid "Fixed absolute tolerances"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:118
msgid "`ApproxEq` uses $10^{-12}$ for `Double` and $10^{-6}$ for `Float`. These are about $9000u$ and $17u$ for the unit roundoff $u$ of each format ($2^{-53}$ and $2^{-24}$), so they suit values of order one, such as entries of normalized vectors. For other scales, compare with an explicit tolerance in your own code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:125
msgid "For `Float` and `Double`, `checked_div(x, y, ctx)` returns `Ok(v)` exactly when IEEE division returns a value that is not produced from an invalid or divide-by-zero exception, and then `v` equals the IEEE quotient bit for bit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:128
msgid "`checked_sqrt(x, ctx)` returns `Ok(√x)` for $x \\ge 0$ and for NaN, and `Err` for $x < 0$. Note that $-0.0 \\ge 0$ holds, so `checked_sqrt(-0.0)` is `Ok(-0.0)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:131
msgid "`checked_compare` is antisymmetric on its domain: `checked_compare(a, b) == Ok(k)` implies `checked_compare(b, a) == Ok(-k)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:133
msgid "`approx_eq` is reflexive and symmetric for non-NaN values and false for NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:137
msgid "**A local `Real` or `Number` trait** combining all operations. It would hide which operations an algorithm uses and force exotic scalars to implement everything."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:140
msgid "**Making `approx_eq` part of `Eq`.** `Eq` must be an equivalence relation; approximate equality is not transitive."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:142
msgid "**Relative tolerances in `ApproxEq`.** They need a policy near zero that depends on the application; the trait stays simple and documented instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/arithmetic.md:147
msgid "`arithmetic` defines no vector, matrix or backend types and does not depend on the other packages of this repository. It does not define algebraic structure traits (those come from `luna-generic`), does not choose tolerances for matrix algorithms (that is the `Tolerance` trait of [`mutable`](mutable.md)), and does not implement arbitrary-precision arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:1
msgid "backends/default design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:5
msgid "The `algebra` traits are only useful if some real dense type implements them. `backends/default` provides that reference backend by wrapping the concrete `@mutable` and `@immut` types, while keeping those concrete packages free of any dependency on the experimental `algebra` layer. It is a backend, not the centre of the ecosystem: generic algorithms depend on the traits, and this package is one way to satisfy them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:14
msgid "Instances are evidence of laws"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:16
msgid "Implementing `@algebra.MatMulMatrix` for a type claims the laws listed in the [algebra design](../algebra.md): associativity and distributivity of `*` over `+` wherever defined. For the wrappers these laws are inherited from the wrapped types, because every operator is defined as \"unwrap, apply the inner operator, wrap\":"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:26
msgid "So `wrap` is a homomorphism for every operation, and any equation that holds for the inner type holds for the wrapper."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:29
msgid "Partiality of the dense product"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:31
msgid "For runtime-shaped dense matrices the product is defined on $\\{(A, B) : \\operatorname{cols}(A) = \\operatorname{rows}(B)\\}$. The trait method returns `Self`, so outside that set the wrapper must do something; it aborts, as the inner type does. This is the documented runtime precondition that the `MatMulMatrix` contract asks implementations to state."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:37
msgid "Dot product and its rounding error"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:39
msgid "`dot` computes $s_n = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} u_i v_i$ by the recurrence $s_0 = 0$, $s_i = s_{i-1} + u_i v_i$. In floating point each step multiplies the error of all earlier terms by another factor $(1 + \\delta)$, and the standard argument gives"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:49
msgid "The relative error is therefore small when the terms have the same sign, and can be large when $\\sum |u_i v_i| \\gg |s_n|$ (cancellation). `matvec` is $m$ such dot products and inherits the same bound row by row."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:55
msgid "Owned wrapper types"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:57
msgid "**Problem.** MoonBit allows `impl Trait for Type` only in the package that owns the trait or the type. Implementing `@algebra.MatMulMatrix` for `@mutable.Matrix` would have to happen in `algebra` (which must not know concrete types) or in `mutable` (which would then depend on the experimental `algebra` layer)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:63
msgid "**Decision.** Define new types `DenseMatrix[T]`, `DenseVector[T]` and their immutable counterparts in this package, each a struct with one public field `inner`, and implement the traits for them here."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:67
msgid "**Why.** The wrapper is owned by the package that implements the traits, so the rule is satisfied, and the dependency direction stays `backends/default → algebra, immut, mutable`. The cost is one indirection in the type, removed by `inner()` and `from_backend` without copying."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:72
msgid "Backend methods for scalar-valued maps"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:74
msgid "`dot`, `scale`, `axpy` and `matvec` are methods of the wrappers, not traits: they involve the scalar type explicitly, which the `algebra` traits cannot name (see the algebra design). Keeping them as methods lets each backend choose its own constraints, for example `AddMonoid + Mul` for `dot` on the mutable vector and `Zero + Add + Mul` on the immutable one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:80
msgid "`axpy` returns a new vector"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:82
msgid "The BLAS routine `axpy` updates $y \\leftarrow a x + y$ in place. Here `x.axpy(a, y)` returns $x a + y$ as a new vector for both wrappers, so the mutable and immutable backends share one value-returning contract. In-place updates remain available on the inner `@mutable.Vector`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:87
msgid "Scalars multiply on the right"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:89
msgid "`scale` uses `right_scale`, computing $v_i a$. For commutative scalars this is the same as $a v_i$; for non-commutative scalars the choice is visible, and it is documented rather than hidden."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:95
msgid "**Homomorphism.** `inner(a op b) == inner(a) op inner(b)` for every operator, so the wrappers satisfy exactly the laws of the wrapped types."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:97
msgid "**No copies on wrapping.** `from_backend(x).inner()` is physically `x`; writes to a mutable inner value are visible through the wrapper."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:99
msgid "**Transpose.** `transpose` materializes; it never returns a view, so the `TransposeMatrix` law $(A^{\\mathsf T})^{\\mathsf T} = A$ holds as values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:101
msgid "**Complexity.** `+`, `-`, `scale`: $O(n)$; `dot`: $n$ multiply-adds; `matvec`: $mn$; `*`: $rcn$ multiply-adds; `transpose`: $O(rc)$ copies."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:106
msgid "**A runtime backend selector** inside one matrix type. It would make every operation branch on the backend and hide which kernel runs. Backends are chosen by type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:109
msgid "**Implementing the traits in `immut` and `mutable` directly.** That would tie the stable concrete packages to the experimental `algebra` layer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:111
msgid "**In-place `axpy`.** It would give the two wrappers different semantics for the same name."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backends/default.md:116
msgid "`backends/default` defines no new traits and no new numerical algorithms; the decompositions, inverses and statistics of [`mutable`](../mutable.md) are reached through `inner()`. It does not provide sparse, lazy, static-size or GPU backends; those should implement the `algebra` traits for their own types rather than convert into these wrappers. The native OpenBLAS backend that once sat beside it is withdrawn in this release and preserved in `contrib/openblas_backend`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:1
msgid "consistency design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:5
msgid "`immut` and `mutable` implement the same core operations twice, with different storage and different kernels, and `MatrixFn` implements them a third time lazily. The `consistency` package checks that these implementations denote the same mathematics, so that users can switch representations without changing results, and so that a kernel optimization in one package cannot silently change semantics."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:14
msgid "Agreement as a homomorphism property"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:16
msgid "Let $\\iota : \\texttt{@immut.Matrix[T]} \\to \\texttt{@mutable.Matrix[T]}$ be the conversion that keeps shape and entries. The packages agree on an operation $\\omega$ when $\\iota$ commutes with it:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:24
msgid "The tests compare both sides through a common observation (`to_array`, `to_2d_array` or `to_string`), which is injective on matrices of a known shape."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:27
msgid "Laws instead of examples"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:29
msgid "Besides agreement, the tests check algebraic laws in both packages: the identity laws, $(AB)^{\\mathsf T} = B^{\\mathsf T} A^{\\mathsf T}$, associativity of the product, distributivity, and $\\operatorname{tr}(A^{\\mathsf T}) = \\operatorname{tr}(A)$. The transpose product law needs commuting scalars; the tests use `Int`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:34
msgid "Why small integers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:36
msgid "All checks use `Int`. Integer arithmetic is the exact ring $\\mathbb{Z}/2^{32}\\mathbb{Z}$, even on overflow, so every law can be tested with `==` and every failure is a real disagreement. With `Double`, different summation orders legitimately differ by rounding (see the [mutable design](mutable.md)), and the tests would need tolerances that could hide real bugs. The property-based tests draw random $2 \\times 2$ integer matrices with quickcheck and check the laws on each sample."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:46
msgid "A separate package"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:48
msgid "The checks need both `immut` and `mutable`, and neither package should depend on the other. A third package that imports both for tests only keeps the dependency graph clean. Its tests are whitebox tests (`*_wbtest.mbt`) so that they can use unqualified helper names."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:53
msgid "Documented differences are tested too"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:55
msgid "Where the packages intentionally differ (for example `set` mutates in `mutable` and returns a new value in `immut`), a test pins the difference down, so that it stays a decision rather than an accident."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:61
msgid "The package asserts, for all tested inputs: equal results for `+`, `*`, transpose, trace, `pow`, determinant (integer inputs), constructors and conversions; the semiring laws of matrix arithmetic; and the documented behaviour of degenerate shapes such as $2 \\times 0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:68
msgid "**Floating-point agreement tests.** They would need tolerances and test rounding rather than semantics; numerical accuracy is tested inside `mutable` instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:71
msgid "**Testing only examples.** Random inputs find disagreements on values the author did not think of, such as negative entries and zeros."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:76
msgid "The package has no public API and is not published for use. It does not test numerical routines that exist in only one package (inverse, Cholesky, eigenvalues) or performance."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:1
msgid "container design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:5
msgid "Generic code often needs to move data between matrix and vector representations, or to look at individual entries, without caring about storage: dense arrays, persistent vectors, views, lazy functions, foreign buffers. `container` describes those structural capabilities separately from the mathematical ones of [`algebra`](algebra.md), so that a type can take part in data movement without claiming algebraic laws, and the other way round."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:14
msgid "A container is a representation of a function"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:16
msgid "Abstractly, a vector of length $n$ with elements in $T$ is a function $v : [n] \\to T$, where $[n] = \\{0, \\dots, n-1\\}$, and an $r \\times c$ matrix is a function $m : [r] \\times [c] \\to T$. A concrete container type `V` *represents* such functions. Two capabilities connect the representation with the function it denotes:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:22
msgid "**read** gives the denotation: $\\llbracket v \\rrbracket(i) = \\mathtt{get}(v, i)$ for $i < \\mathtt{length}(v)$;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:24
msgid "**build** goes the other way: $\\mathtt{tabulate}(n, f)$ is some representation of $f$ restricted to $[n]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:27
msgid "The basic law tying them together is that building and then reading returns the function you started from:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:35
msgid "Reading and then building gives a container that is *observationally* equal to the original: indistinguishable through `get`, though perhaps a different value in memory."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:39
msgid "Generic algorithms as compositions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:41
msgid "With these two maps, the generic algorithms are compositions of functions on the denotations:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:51
msgid "Two laws follow directly and are what the tests check:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:62
msgid "where $\\simeq$ is observational equality. The first pair are the functor laws: on denotations, `map` is post-composition, and post-composition preserves identities and composition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:66
msgid "Editing as a lens"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:68
msgid "A persistent edit $\\mathtt{set}(v, i, x)$ and a read $\\mathtt{get}(v, i)$ form a lens on position $i$, and a correct implementation satisfies the three lens laws for every valid index:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:83
msgid "and, being persistent, it leaves $v$ itself unchanged. A mutable edit satisfies the same equations with \"the state of $v$ after the call\" in place of the returned value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:89
msgid "Operation dictionaries instead of traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:91
msgid "**Problem.** A capability such as \"read elements of type `T` from container `V`\" relates two types. MoonBit traits have one `Self` parameter and no associated types."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:95
msgid "**Options.** (a) A trait on `V` that fixes the element type, for example through a generic method. (b) A trait on `V` per element type. (c) A record of functions parametrized by both types."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:99
msgid "**Decision.** (c): `VectorReadOps[V, T]` and its siblings are plain structs of closures, built with `new` and passed explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:102
msgid "**Why.** A record expresses the two-parameter relation directly. It also lets one container type publish several dictionaries, for example a checked and a clamping read, or dictionaries for different element types of a polymorphic foreign handle, which a trait instance (unique per type) could not. The cost is explicit passing; the algorithms take the dictionaries as arguments."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:108
msgid "Read and build are separate"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:110
msgid "A view can be read but not built; a write-only sink can be built but not read; a foreign handle may allow only reads. Combining read and build into one capability would force every such type either to fake the missing half or to stay out. The algorithms state exactly which half they need on each side: source read, target build."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:116
msgid "Two editing models"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:118
msgid "Persistent editing returns a new value; mutable editing changes the argument and returns `Unit`. They are different contracts: generic code written for the persistent form may keep the old value and expect it unchanged, which a mutable implementation would violate. So they are separate records, and a type provides the one matching its ownership model. Neither implies resizing, insertion or deletion."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:125
msgid "Read everything, then build"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:127
msgid "The algorithms read the whole source into a temporary array before calling `tabulate`. This costs $O(n)$ memory, but it makes failure atomic: if any read fails, the algorithm returns that error before the target exists, so callers never see a half-built container. It also calls the user's mapping function exactly once per element, in row-major order, which matters when the function has effects or is expensive."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:134
msgid "Checked everywhere"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:136
msgid "Every dictionary function returns a `Result`. A generic algorithm cannot know the bounds behaviour of an arbitrary container, so the contract requires each implementation to report bad indices and shapes as values instead of aborting. The repository adapters validate before they touch storage."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:143
msgid "**Shape preservation.** `map` and `convert` preserve $(r, c)$ exactly, including $0 \\times n$ and $n \\times 0$; `transpose` produces $(c, r)$. The algorithms never call `get` on an empty dimension."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:146
msgid "**Index mapping of the transpose.** The source is buffered in row-major order, so source entry $(j, i)$ sits at offset $j c + i$. The target initializer at $(i, j)$ reads that offset, which is exactly $\\llbracket m \\rrbracket(j, i)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:150
msgid "**Error precedence.** A negative reported shape gives `NegativeDimension` before any read; otherwise the first failing read (in row-major order) is returned; otherwise the result of `tabulate` is returned unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:153
msgid "**Complexity.** $n$ reads, one `tabulate` that evaluates the initializer $n$ times, and $n$ calls of the mapping function, for $n = r c$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:158
msgid "**Generic `insert`, `delete`, `create` or `remove`.** Removing a sparse entry, setting it to zero, deleting a row and resizing a matrix are different operations; one name for all of them would have no clear law."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:161
msgid "**Optimized kernel operations in the read dictionary** (row swaps, scaled row additions). Requiring them would exclude simple containers. A future `MatrixKernelOps`-style dictionary may be added beside read and build as an optional capability."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:165
msgid "**Streaming algorithms without the buffer.** They would save memory but give up failure atomicity for targets that are built incrementally."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container.md:170
msgid "`container` defines no storage, no arithmetic and no algebraic laws. It provides no sparse or lazy containers itself, no resizing or structural editing, and no high-performance kernels; the algorithms are $O(n)$ copies through closures and are meant for interchange, not inner loops. Concrete dictionaries for this repository's types live in [`container/adapters`](container/adapters.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:1
msgid "container/adapters design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:5
msgid "The [`container`](../container.md) package defines capabilities without depending on any concrete type. Somebody has to provide the dictionaries for the repository's own types, and that code needs to see both the capability records and every concrete package. `container/adapters` is that place: a leaf package that depends on everything it adapts, so that nothing else has to."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:13
msgid "An adapter is the evidence that a concrete type *represents* the abstract objects of the container model (see the [container design](../container.md)). For a type `V` it supplies the denotation $\\llbracket\\cdot\\rrbracket : V \\to ([n] \\to T)$ through `length` and `get`, and, where possible, a section of it through `tabulate`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:23
msgid "For a view, the denotation is computed from the underlying matrix $A$ of shape $(r, c)$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:32
msgid "A view has no `tabulate`: building would have to create an underlying matrix, and the result would no longer be a view of anything the caller holds. The mutable edit of a view writes through to $A$, which is exactly what a view is for."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:39
msgid "A separate leaf package"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:41
msgid "**Problem.** If `container` contained the adapters, it would depend on `immut`, `mutable` and `backends/default`, and every external library that only wants the capability records would pull in all concrete types."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:45
msgid "**Decision.** The records and algorithms stay in `container`, which depends only on `error`. The adapters live in `container/adapters`, which depends on `container`, `immut`, `mutable` and `backends/default`. No package of the repository depends on the adapters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:50
msgid "**Why.** Dependencies then point from specific to general. External libraries publish their own dictionaries against `container` alone, following the [integration guide](../../integration/container.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:54
msgid "One factory per type and capability"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:56
msgid "Each factory has a name of the form `<type>_<capability>_ops` and returns a new record. Generic functions cannot be stored as values with free type parameters, so a factory function is the way to provide \"the read dictionary for `@immut.Matrix[T]`, for every `T`\". The factories have no constraints on `T`, because reading, building and editing never inspect elements."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:62
msgid "Editing model follows ownership"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:64
msgid "Immutable types and wrappers get persistent edits; mutable types, views and the mutable wrappers get mutable edits. Offering a persistent edit for a mutable matrix would require a full copy per `set`, which hides an $O(rc)$ cost behind an $O(1)$-looking call; offering a mutable edit for an immutable matrix is impossible without breaking its value semantics."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:70
msgid "Validate before delegating"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:72
msgid "The concrete types abort on bad indices. Each adapter checks the index or shape first and returns the error value, so the dictionaries satisfy the panic-free contract of `container` even though the methods they call do not."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:78
msgid "Every read adapter satisfies $\\mathtt{get}(v, i) = \\mathrm{Ok}(v[i])$ on valid indices and `IndexOutOfBounds` elsewhere; builders satisfy the `tabulate`/`get` law above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:81
msgid "Builders call the initializer exactly once per entry, in row-major order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:82
msgid "Persistent edits never modify their argument: `@immut` updates are path copies of a persistent vector."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:84
msgid "Mutable edits of views modify the underlying matrix and nothing else."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:85
msgid "Every factory runs in $O(1)$; the dictionary calls cost what the underlying method costs ($O(1)$ for `@mutable` reads, $O(\\log_{32} n)$ for `@immut`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:90
msgid "**Adapters inside each concrete package.** `immut` and `mutable` would then depend on the experimental `container` layer, which would make the stable concrete APIs inherit its instability."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:93
msgid "**Adapters for external types.** This repository contains adapters only for the types it maintains; external libraries own theirs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/container/adapters.md:98
msgid "The package adds no capability and no algorithm, only evidence for existing types. It provides no build dictionaries for views, no persistent edits for mutable types, and no adapters for types outside this repository. The withdrawn OpenBLAS backend's adapters are preserved with it in `contrib/openblas_backend` and are not part of this package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:1
msgid "error design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:5
msgid "Many matrix operations are partial: a product needs composable shapes, a determinant needs a square matrix, an inverse needs a non-singular one. The `error` package gives these failures one shared, structured representation, so that every checked API of the repository can turn a partial operation into a total one and callers can decide what to do on the failure path without parsing text."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:14
msgid "Partial functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:16
msgid "A partial function $f : X \\rightharpoonup Y$ is a function defined on a subset $\\operatorname{dom} f \\subseteq X$. Matrix operations are partial in the shape or in the values:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:20
#: manual/design/immut.md:191
msgid "Operation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:20
msgid "Domain"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:23
msgid "$A$ square"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:24
msgid "$A$ square, $k \\ge 0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:25
msgid ""
"$A$ square and $\\det A \\n"
"e 0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:26
msgid "$A$ has at least one entry"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:28
msgid "Two ways to make them total"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:30
msgid "A program must do *something* on every input. There are two honest choices."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:32
msgid "The **checked** form extends $f$ to all of $X$ with an error value:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:42
msgid ""
"The **unchecked** form keeps the type $X \\to Y$ and makes membership in the domain a precondition: on $x \\n"
"otin \\operatorname{dom} f$ it aborts (or, for some routines documented as such, returns an unspecified value)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:46
msgid "The two forms are related by the law that the repository maintains for every checked/unchecked pair:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:57
msgid "In the code, almost every checked method is literally \"validate, then call the unchecked method\", which makes the first implication hold by construction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:60
msgid "Composition"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:62
msgid "Checked operations compose in the Kleisli category of the error monad: given $\\hat f : X \\to Y + E$ and $\\hat g : Y \\to Z + E$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:73
msgid "and the domain of the composite is $\\{x \\in \\operatorname{dom} f : f(x) \\in \\operatorname{dom} g\\}$. In MoonBit this is a `match` that returns early on `Err`, or `Result::bind`. A single error type across the repository is what makes this composition possible without conversions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:81
msgid "A struct with a kind, not a `suberror`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:83
msgid "**Options.** (a) A MoonBit `suberror` raised with `raise`. (b) A plain enum. (c) A struct holding a kind and a message, returned in `Result`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:86
msgid "**Decision.** (c), following the Luna-Flow convention: `pub struct LinearAlgebraError { kind, message }` with `pub enum LinearAlgebraErrorKind`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:89
msgid "**Why.** A `Result` is an ordinary value: it can be stored, mapped and combined, and its presence is visible in the signature. The kind is the contract for control flow; the message is free to change and carries details such as which index was wrong. Keeping the enum read-only outside the package and exposing snake_case constructors (`LinearAlgebraError::singular_matrix`) lets the package add fields later without breaking callers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:96
msgid "Predicates beside the enum"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:98
msgid "Every kind has an `is_*` predicate. Most callers only need a yes/no question (\"was it singular?\"), and a predicate keeps working when a kind gains a payload in a future version, where an exhaustive `match` would not."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:102
msgid "Unchecked forms keep their old behaviour"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:104
msgid "Before `0.4.0` the matrix methods aborted or returned `Option`. Those behaviours are preserved under explicit `unchecked_*` names (`unchecked_inverse` still returns `Option`), and the short names became the checked forms. A reader of `m.inverse()` therefore sees the safe form by default, and the unsafe one announces itself."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:110
msgid "Reserved kinds"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:112
msgid "`InvalidLength`, `RaggedRows`, `NonConvergence` and `ArithmeticFailure` are not produced by any API in this release; the corresponding operations (`from_array`, `from_2d_array`, `eigen`) still abort. The kinds exist so that downstream checked wrappers and future checked constructors can report these failures in the shared vocabulary without a breaking change to the enum."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:118
msgid "No `Show` or `Debug`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:120
msgid "The error implements only `Eq`. Formatting and localization are presentation policy, which this package leaves to applications; `message` is the diagnostic text."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:126
msgid "**Kind determines the predicate.** For every error `e` exactly one `is_*` predicate returns `true`, the one named after `e.kind`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:128
msgid "**Checked/unchecked law.** For every pair in `immut` and `mutable`, the law of the background section holds; the [`consistency`](consistency.md) and package tests exercise both paths."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:131
msgid "**Deterministic error choice.** When an input violates several preconditions, the checks run in a fixed order, so the reported kind is a function of the input. For example `pow` checks squareness before the sign of the exponent: a non-square matrix with a negative exponent always reports `NonSquareMatrix`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:136
msgid "**No partial effects.** Checked operations of the immutable package never modify their arguments; checked operations of the mutable package validate before they write, so an `Err` result leaves the arguments unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:142
msgid "**Error codes as strings** (an older `E_`-prefixed convention mentioned in contributor notes). Strings cannot be matched exhaustively and invite parsing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:144
msgid "**`Option` for every failure.** It loses the reason; `None` from an inverse cannot say whether the matrix was non-square or singular."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:146
msgid "**Separate error types per package.** Composition across `immut`, `mutable` and `container` would need conversions at every boundary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/error.md:151
msgid "`error` defines values only. It implements no matrix algorithm, no recovery strategy for singular or ill-conditioned input, no logging and no formatting. It does not decide which operations are checked; that is the responsibility of each package that returns a `LinearAlgebraError`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:1
msgid "immut design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:5
msgid "`immut` gives linear algebra with value semantics: a matrix or vector, once built, never changes, and every operation returns a new value. Programs can then keep old versions, share values freely between components and reason about code by substitution. The package also aims to compute *exactly* whenever the scalar type allows it, so that integer and big-integer matrices get exact determinants and powers rather than floating-point approximations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:14
msgid "Value semantics and referential transparency"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:16
msgid "An expression is referentially transparent when it can be replaced by its value without changing the program. With immutable matrices,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:24
msgid "so any expression mentioning $A$ means the same thing on either side of the update. Algebraic identities can then be used directly as program transformations: $(A + B) + C$ and $A + (B + C)$ denote the same value for exact scalars, and an intermediate result may be reused or recomputed at will."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:29
msgid "Persistent storage"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:31
msgid "Entries live in row-major order in `moonbitlang/core/immut/vector`, a persistent vector implemented as a trie with branching factor 32. Replacing one element copies the path from the root to the leaf and shares every other node, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:41
msgid "and the old matrix stays valid. Reads also cost $O(\\log_{32} N)$; at most seven levels cover $2^{32}$ elements. Whole-matrix operations rebuild the trie in $O(N)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:45
msgid "Matrix powers by repeated squaring"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:47
msgid "For a square matrix over a semiring, write $k = \\sum_t b_t 2^t$ in binary. Then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:53
msgid "which needs at most $\\lfloor \\log_2 k \\rfloor$ squarings and as many extra products, instead of $k - 1$ products. The rearrangement of the product is valid because matrix multiplication is associative over any semiring (see the [algebra design](algebra.md)); commutativity of the scalars is not needed, since all factors are powers of the same $A$. `pow` keeps a state $S$, an exponent $e$ and a base $B$ with the invariant"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:64
msgid "initially $(I, k, A)$; each step either multiplies $S$ by $B$ when $e$ is odd, then halves $e$ and squares $B$. The invariant is preserved, and when $e = 0$ the state is $A^k$. For fixed-width integers the result is exact in $\\mathbb{Z}/2^{32}\\mathbb{Z}$ even when it overflows, because wrapping arithmetic *is* the ring arithmetic of that quotient."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:70
msgid "Fraction-free determinant"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:72
msgid "Gaussian elimination over a field computes $\\det A$ as the product of pivots but divides at every step, which leaves the integers. Bareiss' algorithm keeps every intermediate value an integer.[^bareiss] Let $a^{(-1)}_{-1,-1} = 1$, $a^{(0)}_{ij} = a_{ij}$, and for $k = 0, 1, \\dots, n-2$ and $i, j > k$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:82
msgid "By Sylvester's determinant identity, each $a^{(k)}_{ij}$ equals the minor of $A$ formed by rows $0, \\dots, k-1, i$ and columns $0, \\dots, k-1, j$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:95
msgid "Two consequences follow. First, the division in the recurrence is *exact*: the numerator is a multiple of the previous pivot, so over an integral domain such as $\\mathbb{Z}$ the quotient is again in the domain. Second, the last value is the full determinant, $a^{(n-1)}_{n-1,n-1} = \\det A$. The proof of the identity is in the attachment below."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:101
msgid "[Bareiss elimination: exactness and correctness](../../attachments/design_immut_bareiss.typ)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:103
msgid "Pivoting fits in without breaking exactness. Row $i$ of $a^{(k)}$ depends only on row $i$ of $A$ and on rows $0, \\dots, k-1$, so exchanging two rows with indices $\\ge k$ before step $k$ is the same as running the algorithm on $A$ with those rows exchanged, which multiplies the final result by $-1$. If the whole column below the pivot is zero, those minors vanish, the rows are linearly dependent, and $\\det A = 0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:110
msgid "Every intermediate value is a minor of $A$, so Hadamard's inequality bounds them all:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:117
msgid "over the $k + 1$ rows involved. The numerator of the recurrence is a difference of two products of such minors, so it is bounded by twice the square of that product. This gives an overflow criterion for `Int`: if $H = \\prod_r \\max(1, \\lVert \\text{row}_r \\rVert_2)$ satisfies $2H^2 < 2^{31}$, no intermediate value overflows and the result is exact (for `Int64`, $2H^2 < 2^{63}$). For `BigInt` the algorithm is always exact, every intermediate integer is bounded by $2H^2$, and it uses $O(n^3)$ arithmetic operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:126
msgid "E. H. Bareiss, \"Sylvester's identity and multistep integer-preserving Gaussian elimination\", *Mathematics of Computation* 22 (1968), 565–578."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:129
msgid "For $n \\le 4$ the package uses closed formulas instead. For $n = 3$ it is the cofactor expansion along the first row; for $n = 4$ it is Laplace's expansion along the first two rows,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:138
msgid "six products of complementary $2 \\times 2$ minors. These formulas use only ring operations, so they are exact for every ring and need no division at all."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:141
msgid "Lazy matrices"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:143
msgid "A `MatrixFn` is a pair $(\\text{shape}, f)$ with $f : [r] \\times [c] \\to T$. Operations compose functions: $\\mathtt{map}(g)$ is $g \\circ f$, transpose is $f \\circ \\mathrm{swap}$, and the product is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:151
msgid "evaluated on demand. Nothing is cached, so the cost of one entry of a product is the inner dimension times the cost of entries of the factors. A product tree of depth $d$ over $n \\times n$ matrices therefore costs $O(n^{d})$ per entry: lazy powers are cheap to build and expensive to read."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:158
msgid "Persistent vector storage"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:160
msgid "**Options.** (a) A copied `Array` per update, (b) a persistent trie, (c) functions only. **Decision.** (b) for `Matrix` and `Vector`, (c) offered separately as `MatrixFn`. **Why.** A copied array makes `set` cost $O(N)$; a trie makes it $O(\\log_{32} N)$ while keeping reads fast and the value immutable. Functions are useful for structured or symbolic matrices, but their read cost depends on the history of operations, so they are a separate type whose cost model is stated rather than hidden."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:168
msgid "Exact algorithms where the scalar allows them"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:170
msgid "`determinant` asks only for `Compare + Num + Div`, not for a field, so it accepts `Int`, `Int64` and `BigInt`. With Bareiss elimination the result is exact over those types; over `Double` it behaves like elimination with scaled pivots. The [`mutable`](mutable.md) package, which targets floating point, uses LU with partial pivoting and a tolerance instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:176
msgid "Checked short names, unchecked explicit names"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:178
msgid "`matmul`, `trace`, `determinant` and `pow` return `Result`; their `unchecked_*` partners abort. The operators `+`, `-`, `*` cannot return `Result` and abort on mismatched shapes. The checked form validates and then calls the unchecked one, which gives the law `checked(x) == Ok(unchecked(x))` on the domain by construction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:184
msgid "Alignment with `mutable`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:186
msgid "Names, argument order and checked/unchecked conventions match `@mutable` wherever both packages offer an operation, and the [`consistency`](consistency.md) tests compare their results. Differences are deliberate and listed here:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:193
msgid "identity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:193
msgid "top-level `identity(n)`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:194
msgid "update"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:194
msgid "`set` returns a new matrix"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:194
msgid "`set` writes in place"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:195
#: manual/design/immut.md:197
msgid "not available"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:195
#: manual/design/immut.md:197
msgid "available"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:196
msgid "determinant"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:196
msgid "Bareiss, exact over integral domains"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:196
msgid "LU with tolerance, floating point"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:197
msgid "decompositions, inverse, statistics"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:198
msgid "not on `Vector` (see `ImmutableDenseVector::dot`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:200
msgid "No subtraction on `Vector`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:202
msgid "`Vector` implements `Add`, `Mul` and `Neg` but not `Sub`; `u - v` is written `u + -v`. This is an asymmetry inherited from earlier releases, not a mathematical statement. The `backends/default` wrappers provide `-`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:208
msgid "**Immutability.** No public operation modifies an existing `Matrix`, `Vector` or `MatrixFn`; `set` and `swap_*` return new values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:210
msgid "**Shape invariants.** $r, c \\ge 0$ and the backing vector has exactly $rc$ elements; constructors reject anything else by aborting."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:212
msgid "**Bounds.** Every public read checks row and column separately, so a column index that would land inside the next row is rejected rather than read."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:214
msgid "**Exactness.** Over `BigInt`, `determinant` and `pow` are exact; over `Int` and `Int64`, `pow` is exact modulo $2^{w}$ and `determinant` is exact unless a minor overflows (see the Hadamard bound)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:217
msgid "**Empty cases.** $\\det$ and $\\operatorname{tr}$ of the $0 \\times 0$ matrix are $1$ and $0$; $A^0 = I$; a product with inner dimension $0$ is the zero matrix."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:220
msgid "**Complexity.** `set`: $O(\\log_{32} N)$; `map`, `+`, `transpose`, `swap_*`: $O(N)$; `matmul`: $O(rcn)$; `determinant`: $O(n^3)$; `pow`: $O(n^3 \\log k)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:226
msgid "**Floating-point LU for `immut` determinants.** It would need a field and a tolerance and would lose exactness for integer matrices, the main reason to use this package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:229
msgid "**A runtime backend selector inside `Matrix`.** Backends are separate types ([`backends/default`](backends/default.md)); `Matrix` has one representation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:231
msgid "**Caching in `MatrixFn`.** It would make a pure value hold mutable state and change the cost model silently. Materialize with `Matrix::make` instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:236
msgid "`immut` does not provide in-place updates, views, inverses, decompositions, eigenvalues, statistics or tolerance-based predicates; those belong to [`mutable`](mutable.md). It does not detect integer overflow, does not offer a checked `MatrixFn` API, and does not implement the [`algebra`](algebra.md) traits itself (the wrappers in `backends/default` do)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:1
msgid "internal design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:5
msgid "`immut` and `mutable` check the same preconditions (squareness, bounds, compatible shapes) and must report them identically. `internal` keeps one implementation of each check, so the two packages cannot drift apart, without exposing the helpers to downstream code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:12
msgid "Each guard is the characteristic test of a domain from the [error design](error.md): $\\operatorname{dom}(\\operatorname{tr}) = \\{A : r = c\\}$, $\\operatorname{dom}(\\cdot) = \\{(A, B) : c_A = r_B\\}$, and so on. Writing the test once as a total function $\\chi : X \\to \\mathrm{Unit} + E$ gives both forms of a partial operation:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:24
msgid "Deriving the aborting guard from the checked one (`ensure_x` matches on `ensure_x_checked`) makes the two agree on every input by construction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:29
msgid "An internal package"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:31
msgid "MoonBit's internal-package rule restricts imports of `Luna-Flow/linear-algebra/internal` to packages of this module. The helpers can therefore change freely, while their effects are specified on the public methods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:36
msgid "`HasShape` separate from `MatrixShape`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:38
msgid "`@algebra.MatrixShape` has the same method, but `algebra` is experimental and the concrete packages should not depend on it. `HasShape` gives the guards a bound that `immut` and `mutable` implement locally. The repetition is the cost of keeping the stable concrete packages independent of the experimental layer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:43
msgid "Row before column"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:45
msgid "`ensure_index_in_bounds` checks the row first, then the column, and each against its own dimension. Checking the flat offset $r c' + c$ against $rc'$ instead would accept a column index that spills into the next row."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:51
msgid "`ensure_x(m)` aborts if and only if `ensure_x_checked(m)` returns `Err`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:52
msgid "Each checked guard returns the error kind listed on the [API page](../api/internal.md), with a fixed message."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:54
msgid "All guards run in $O(1)$ and only call `shape`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:58
msgid "**Duplicating the checks in each package**, which is how bounds behaviour diverged before the `0.4` line."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:60
msgid "**Public helpers.** They would become API that downstream code depends on."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:64
msgid "`internal` checks shapes and indices only. It contains no arithmetic, no storage, and no checks that depend on values (singularity, emptiness of data, convergence)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:1
msgid "mutable design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:5
msgid "`mutable` is the execution-oriented half of the repository. It stores a matrix in one flat row-major array, lets callers update it in place and work through live views, and implements the floating-point numerical routines: determinant, inverse, rank, row reduction, Cholesky factorization, symmetric eigenvalues and the power method. The public API still reads like value operations wherever possible; mutation is confined to methods that return `Unit` and to the views, so callers can tell from a signature whether a call changes its receiver."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:16
msgid "Throughout, $u$ is the unit roundoff ($2^{-53}$ for `Double`, $2^{-24}$ for `Float`), $\\mathrm{fl}(x \\circ y) = (x \\circ y)(1 + \\delta)$ with $|\\delta| \\le u$, and $\\gamma_n = n u / (1 - n u)$. Inequalities between matrices hold entry-wise, and $|A|$ is the matrix of absolute values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:21
msgid "Matrix product"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:23
msgid "$(AB)_{ik} = \\sum_j a_{ij} b_{jk}$ costs $rcn$ multiply-adds. The kernels sum in different orders (four partial products per step in the unrolled kernels; a packed copy of the columns of $B$ for products of at least $4 \\times 16 \\times 16$), but every order satisfies"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:32
msgid "so results on different targets agree to that accuracy, not bit for bit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:34
msgid "LU factorization with partial pivoting"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:36
msgid "For square $A$, Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting computes a permutation $P$, a unit lower triangular $L$ and an upper triangular $U$ with"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:43
msgid "At step $k$ it chooses the row $p \\ge k$ with the largest $|a^{(k)}_{pk}|$, swaps it into position $k$, and for $i > k$ stores the multiplier $l_{ik} = a^{(k)}_{ik} / a^{(k)}_{kk}$ and updates $a^{(k+1)}_{ij} = a^{(k)}_{ij} - l_{ik} a^{(k)}_{kj}$. Pivoting guarantees $|l_{ik}| \\le 1$. The cost is $\\tfrac23 n^3$ flops."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:49
msgid "**Determinant.** Taking determinants of $PA = LU$ with $\\det L = 1$ and $\\det P = (-1)^{s}$ for $s$ exchanges,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:56
msgid "**Solving.** $Ax = b$ becomes $L y = P b$ (forward substitution) and $U x = y$ (back substitution), each $n^2$ flops per right-hand side. The inverse is the solution for the $n$ columns of $I$: $\\tfrac23 n^3 + n \\cdot 2n^2 = \\tfrac83 n^3$ flops."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:61
msgid "**Stability.** The computed factors satisfy the backward error bound (Wilkinson; Higham, Theorem 9.3)[^higham]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:68
msgid "With $|l_{ik}| \\le 1$ this gives $\\lVert \\Delta A \\rVert_\\infty \\le n \\gamma_n \\rho_n \\lVert A \\rVert_\\infty$, where $\\rho_n = \\max_{i,j,k} |a^{(k)}_{ij}| / \\max_{i,j} |a_{ij}|$ is the growth factor. Partial pivoting bounds $\\rho_n \\le 2^{n-1}$; the bound is attained only by contrived matrices, and in practice $\\rho_n$ is small, so the method is backward stable in practice. The *forward* error of a solve is then governed by the condition number: $\\lVert \\hat x - x \\rVert / \\lVert x \\rVert \\lesssim \\kappa(A)\\, n \\gamma_n \\rho_n$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:77
msgid "N. J. Higham, *Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms*, 2nd ed., SIAM, 2002, chapters 9 (LU), 10 (Cholesky) and 8 (triangular systems)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:81
msgid "Closed forms for small determinants"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:83
msgid "For $n \\le 4$ the determinant is evaluated by formulas instead of LU: the rule $ad - bc$, cofactor expansion along the first row for $n = 3$, and for $n = 4$ Laplace's expansion along the first two rows into six products of complementary $2 \\times 2$ minors,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:92
msgid "They use no division and no pivoting decision, which avoids the tolerance test for tiny matrices. They are not backward stable in the LU sense: for ill-conditioned input the cancellation in $ad - bc$ can lose all relative accuracy, exactly as the determinant itself is ill-conditioned there."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:97
msgid "Rank and reduced row echelon form"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:99
msgid "`rank` runs elimination with partial pivoting on a copy and counts pivots whose magnitude is at least the tolerance. This is the *numerical rank* with an absolute threshold $\\tau$: the number of pivots $\\ge \\tau$. It is exact for matrices whose pivots are well separated from $\\tau$ and otherwise depends on scaling. (The singular value decomposition gives the reliable numerical rank, $\\#\\{\\sigma_i > \\tau\\}$; it is not implemented.)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:106
msgid "`reduce_row_elimination` is Gauss–Jordan elimination: each pivot row is scaled to make the pivot one, and the pivot column is cleared above and below. It costs about $r c \\min(r, c)$ flops and works in place."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:110
msgid "Cholesky factorization"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:112
msgid "A symmetric positive definite (SPD) matrix has a unique factorization $A = L L^{\\mathsf T}$ with $L$ lower triangular and $l_{jj} > 0$. Comparing entries of $A = L L^{\\mathsf T}$ for $i \\ge j$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:123
msgid "The code evaluates these row by row (the Cholesky–Banachiewicz order), $\\tfrac13 n^3$ flops. The radicand at step $j$ equals the pivot of Gaussian elimination without pivoting, which is the ratio of leading principal minors $\\det M_{j+1} / \\det M_j$. By Sylvester's criterion, $A$ is SPD exactly when all leading principal minors are positive, so the factorization succeeds exactly for SPD matrices; this is why `is_positive_definite` is implemented by attempting it. Cholesky needs no pivoting: from $a_{jj} = \\sum_k l_{jk}^2$ every $|l_{jk}| \\le \\sqrt{a_{jj}}$, so entries cannot grow, and the computed factor satisfies $\\hat L \\hat L^{\\mathsf T} = A + \\Delta A$ with $|\\Delta A| \\le \\gamma_{n+1} |\\hat L|\\,|\\hat L^{\\mathsf T}|$ (Higham, Theorem 10.3)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:136
msgid "Symmetric eigenvalue problem"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:138
msgid "For real symmetric $A$ the spectral theorem gives $A = Q \\Lambda Q^{\\mathsf T}$ with $Q$ orthogonal and $\\Lambda$ real diagonal. `eigen` computes it in two phases."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:142
msgid "**Householder tridiagonalization.** A Householder reflector $H = I - 2 v v^{\\mathsf T} / v^{\\mathsf T} v$ is symmetric and orthogonal and can map a vector onto a multiple of a coordinate vector. Applying $n - 2$ reflectors from both sides,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:151
msgid "where $T$ is symmetric tridiagonal with diagonal $d$ and off-diagonal $e$. The code accumulates $Q_1$ explicitly; together about $\\tfrac83 n^3$ flops. Each row is scaled by the sum of its absolute values before the reflector is formed, which avoids overflow and underflow in $\\lVert x \\rVert_2$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:156
msgid "**Implicit QL with Wilkinson shifts.** The tridiagonal $T$ is diagonalized by plane rotations. Before each sweep on the unreduced block starting at $l$, the shift $\\sigma$ is the eigenvalue of the leading $2 \\times 2$ block $\\begin{pmatrix} d_l & e_l \\\\ e_l & d_{l+1} \\end{pmatrix}$ closer to $d_l$. With $g = (d_{l+1} - d_l)/(2 e_l)$ that block has eigenvalues"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:166
msgid "and the one closer to $d_l$ is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:173
msgid "the second form, used by the code, avoids cancellation. A sweep applies Givens rotations that chase the resulting bulge and update $Q$ with the same rotations. An off-diagonal entry is treated as zero when"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:181
msgid "a test that is relative for large diagonal entries and absolute near zero. Convergence with the Wilkinson shift is cubic for symmetric tridiagonal matrices in practice and is never observed to fail on finite input; the code nevertheless aborts after 60 sweeps for one eigenvalue. The whole procedure is a product of orthogonal transformations and is backward stable: the computed eigenvalues are exact for $A + \\Delta A$ with $\\lVert \\Delta A \\rVert_2 = O(u)\\lVert A \\rVert_2$, so by Weyl's inequality"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:193
msgid "Eigenvectors are accurate in proportion to $u\\lVert A \\rVert / \\text{gap}$, where the gap is the distance to the nearest other eigenvalue."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:196
msgid "**The $2 \\times 2$ case.** For $A = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}$ the characteristic polynomial $\\lambda^2 - (a + d)\\lambda + (ad - bc)$ gives, with $m = (a + d)/2$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:204
msgid ""
"For $b \\n"
"e 0$ the vector $(b, \\lambda - a)^{\\mathsf T}$ is an eigenvector:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:213
msgid "since $(\\lambda - a)(\\lambda - d) = \\lambda^2 - (a + d)\\lambda + ad = bc$ by the characteristic equation. The code returns these vectors without normalizing them. When $|\\lambda_2| \\ll |\\lambda_1|$, the subtraction $m - \\sqrt{\\cdot}$ cancels and $\\lambda_2$ loses relative accuracy; $\\lambda_2 = \\det A / \\lambda_1$ would be the stable alternative."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:219
msgid "Power method"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:221
msgid "Starting from $x_0 = (1, \\dots, 1)$ (or a coordinate vector if $A x_0 = 0$), the method iterates"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:229
msgid ""
"and stops when $\\lVert A x_k - \\lambda_k x_k \\rVert_\\infty \\le \\tau$. If $A$ is diagonalizable with eigenvalues $|\\lambda_1| > |\\lambda_2| \\ge \\dots$ and $x_0$ has a component $c_1 \\n"
"e 0$ along the eigenvector $v_1$, then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:237
msgid ""
"so the direction converges linearly with ratio $|\\lambda_2 / \\lambda_1|$, and for symmetric $A$ the Rayleigh quotient converges with ratio $|\\lambda_2 / \\lambda_1|^2$. When $|\\lambda_1| = |\\lambda_2|$ with $\\lambda_1 \\n"
"e \\lambda_2$ (for example $\\pm 1$) the direction oscillates and the residual test never passes, and when $A$ is nilpotent the iterate reaches zero; both cases return `None`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:244
msgid "Statistics"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:246
msgid "`variance` is the population variance computed in two passes: first the mean $\\bar a$, then $\\tfrac1N \\sum (a_i - \\bar a)^2$. The one-pass formula $\\tfrac1N \\sum a_i^2 - \\bar a^2$ subtracts two nearly equal numbers when the data have a large mean and a small spread, and can even return a negative value; the two-pass form sums non-negative terms whose rounding error is relative to the variance itself (Chan, Golub and LeVeque, 1983). The count $N$ is accumulated as a sum of ones in `T`, exact up to $2^{53}$ entries for `Double` and $2^{24}$ for `Float`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:255
msgid "Transpose views and products"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:257
msgid "`Transpose::mul` computes $A^{\\mathsf T} B^{\\mathsf T}$ as $(BA)^{\\mathsf T}$ by reusing the matrix kernel on the wrapped matrices. Entry by entry,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:265
msgid "which agree when the scalars commute. All scalar types with `Tolerance` (`Float`, `Double`) commute, but `Transpose::mul` only requires `AddMonoid + Mul`; for a non-commutative scalar type the result is the product in the wrong order (see the [algebra design](algebra.md))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:272
msgid "Flat row-major storage"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:274
msgid "**Options.** An array of row arrays, a persistent structure, or one flat array. **Decision.** One `Array[T]` with entry $(i, j)$ at $i c + j$, shared by all four targets. **Why.** It gives $O(1)$ access with one bounds check per coordinate, contiguous rows for the inner loops of elimination and products, and zero-cost row and column views. `from_array` adopts the caller's array so that large inputs need not be copied; the price is aliasing, which the API documents."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:282
msgid "Views instead of copies"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:284
msgid "`row_view`, `col_view` and `to_transpose` return live views in $O(1)$. A view is a pair (matrix, index) or a wrapper, so writes go to the shared storage and no synchronization is needed. Materializing is always explicit (`to_vector`, `materialize`, `transpose`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:289
msgid "Target-specific kernels with shared semantics"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:291
msgid "The package has one source file per target for the matrix, LU, view and transpose code. They differ only in loop structure (unrolling, packing, avoidance of division in index computations) chosen for each backend; the public semantics, including bounds and error behaviour, are identical, and the tests run on all four targets. Floating-point results may differ in the last bits because summation orders differ."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:298
msgid "Tolerance-based decisions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:300
msgid "Elimination must decide when a computed pivot \"is zero\". The package uses one absolute threshold $\\tau$ = `Tolerance::tolerance()` = $10^{-11}$ for `Double` and `Float`, applied as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:304
msgid "Routine"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:306
msgid "LU (`determinant` for $n \\ge 5$, `inverse`, `is_invertible`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:306
msgid "pivot $\\lvert u_{kk}\\rvert < \\tau$ means singular"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:307
msgid "largest remaining $\\lvert a_{ik}\\rvert < \\tau$ means no pivot"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:308
msgid "entries $\\le \\tau$ are set to zero"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:309
msgid "radicand $\\le \\tau$ means not positive definite"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:310
msgid "`is_symmetric`, fast-path detection (identity, diagonal, permutation, triangular)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:311
msgid "`eigen` deflation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:312
msgid "residual $\\lVert Ax - \\lambda x\\rVert_\\infty \\le \\tau$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:314
msgid "An absolute threshold is simple and predictable, but it is not scale invariant. Scaling $A$ by $10^{-12}$ makes every pivot fall below $\\tau$, so a perfectly conditioned matrix is reported singular; scaling by $10^{12}$ lets a numerically singular matrix pass. Scale data to order one before calling these routines. For `Float`, $\\tau = 10^{-11}$ is far below the unit roundoff $\\approx 6 \\times 10^{-8}$, so the tests effectively check for exact zeros, and nearly singular `Float` matrices are not detected. The trait is closed (`pub`, not `pub(open)`), so these two instances are the only ones; a scale-aware tolerance policy is future work and would be a breaking change."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:324
msgid "Fast paths"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:326
msgid "`inverse` recognizes the identity (returns a copy), diagonal matrices (inverts the diagonal) and permutation matrices (returns the transpose, since $P^{\\mathsf T} P = I$ for a matrix whose columns are distinct coordinate vectors). `determinant` recognizes triangular matrices for $n \\ge 5$ and multiplies the diagonal. Each test costs $O(n^2)$ and saves an $O(n^3)$ factorization. The tests use the tolerance, so a matrix that is diagonal up to $\\tau$ is treated as exactly diagonal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:334
msgid "Checked forms without changing the kernels"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:336
msgid "Every checked method validates (squareness, exponent sign, non-emptiness, lengths) and then calls its unchecked partner, which keeps the original aborting or `Option` behaviour. There is no checked matrix product here: `*` validates and aborts, and `unchecked_matmul` does not validate at all. This asymmetry with `@immut.Matrix::matmul` is known; a checked `matmul` would be an addition, not a change."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:343
msgid "Symmetric eigenvalues only"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:345
msgid "General real matrices can have complex eigenvalues, which a function returning `Vector[T]` cannot represent for real `T`. `eigen` therefore accepts only symmetric matrices, for which all eigenvalues are real and an orthonormal eigenbasis exists, and aborts otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:352
msgid "**Storage.** `data.length() == row * col` at all times; every public accessor checks the row and column separately."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:354
msgid "**Value-returning methods do not mutate.** Only `Unit`-returning methods, view writes and `reduce_row_elimination` change a matrix."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:356
msgid "**Checked/unchecked law.** `x.f() == Ok(x.unchecked_f())` whenever the precondition holds; `inverse` returns `Err(SingularMatrix)` exactly when `unchecked_inverse` returns `None`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:359
msgid "**Determinant consistency.** For $n \\ge 5$ and a matrix that is not triangular within $\\tau$, `determinant` returns `0` exactly when the LU factorization reports a pivot below $\\tau$, which is also when `is_invertible` returns `false` and `inverse` fails. For $n \\le 4$ the closed formulas are used for `determinant`, while `is_invertible` still uses LU, so a matrix with a tiny non-zero determinant can be \"not invertible\" with a non-zero `determinant`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:366
msgid "**Residual guarantees.** `cholesky_decomposition` and `eigen` return factors whose reconstruction error is $O(u)\\lVert A \\rVert$; `power_method` returns only pairs with residual at most $\\tau$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:369
msgid "**Complexity.** `*`: $rcn$; `determinant`, `inverse`: $O(n^3)$; `rank`, `reduce_row_elimination`: $O(rc\\min(r, c))$; `cholesky_decomposition`: $n^3/3$; `eigen`: $O(n^3)$; `power_method`: $O(n^2)$ per iteration; statistics: $O(rc)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:376
msgid "**A relative or norm-scaled tolerance.** More robust, but it would change the results of existing callers; deferred until a tolerance policy can be passed explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:379
msgid "**Returning complex eigenvalues.** Would make this package depend on a complex number type and change the signature for real symmetric input."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:381
msgid "**Copy-on-write views.** Would hide the cost of writes and break the in-place contract that views exist for."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:383
msgid "**A single portable kernel.** Measured to be slower on some targets; the per-target files trade code size for speed while sharing one specification."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:388
msgid "`mutable` does not provide linear solves for a right-hand side as a public method (only the inverse), QR, SVD or least-squares solvers, eigenvalues of non-symmetric matrices, sparse storage, condition number estimates, or scale-aware tolerances. It does not implement the [`algebra`](algebra.md) traits itself; [`backends/default`](backends/default.md) wraps it for that. Domain workflows such as regression or optimization belong in downstream packages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:1
msgid "perf design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:5
msgid "`perf` lets the standard `moon bench` tool measure the steady-state cost of the `@mutable` numerical kernels on every registered case, with no extra infrastructure, so that a kernel change can be compared before and after."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:11
msgid "`moon bench` repeats each benchmark body many times and reports statistics of the per-iteration time. A steady-state measurement estimates the expected cost $\\mathbb{E}[T]$ of one call after warm-up, excluding process start, fixture I/O and first-call effects. The repository's report scripts summarize samples with robust statistics: the median, the nearest-rank 90th percentile, and the median absolute deviation $\\operatorname{MAD} = \\operatorname{median}_i |t_i - \\operatorname{median}(t)|$. Unlike the mean and the standard deviation, the median and the MAD have a breakdown point of 50%: a few samples disturbed by the operating system cannot move them arbitrarily."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:23
msgid "Preparation outside the timed region"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:25
msgid "Inputs are prepared once per case before registering the benchmark. Only the kernel call and the checksum fold are inside the timed closure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:28
msgid "Keep the checksum"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:30
msgid "Passing the checksum to `b.keep` makes the result observable, so the compiler cannot eliminate the call, while costing only one 64-bit fold per output value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:33
msgid "Not part of the default gate"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:35
msgid "Benchmarks depend on the machine and take long; they are excluded from `run_test.sh` unless `LINEAR_ALGEBRA_TEST_BENCH=1` is set."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:40
msgid "Every registered case is benchmarked exactly once per run, under a name that identifies operation and case."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:45
msgid "**A hand-written timing loop.** `moon bench` already handles iteration counts and reporting; [`perf_runner`](perf_runner.md) covers the cases where raw samples are needed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:51
msgid "`perf` does not compute or store statistics, does not compare against baselines, and does not include cold-start timing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:1
msgid "perf_runner design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:5
msgid "`moon bench` reports summaries; the repository's report pipeline needs raw per-sample timings of a single case, under its own control of repetition and warm-up, and a way to replay a case and check its result. `perf_runner` is that single-case tool."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:12
msgid "A sample is the mean time of `repeat` consecutive calls, $t = \\tfrac1{\\text{repeat}} \\sum_{j=1}^{\\text{repeat}} T_j$. Averaging inside a sample reduces timer resolution error, which is fixed per measurement, by the factor `repeat`, and reduces independent per-call noise by $1/\\sqrt{\\text{repeat}}$. Across samples the pipeline uses order statistics (median, p90) and the MAD, which are robust to outliers caused by scheduling. Warm-up rounds discard the transient of the first calls (cache, branch predictors, lazy initialization)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:23
msgid "Scratch copies outside the timer"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:25
msgid "For cases that mutate their inputs, `repeat` copies are prepared *before* `monotonic_clock_start`, so copying is not measured and every call sees the same input."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:29
msgid "Checksums in every mode"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:31
msgid "Each call's checksum is folded into a running value, so the work stays observable and the diagnostic mode can verify bit-identical results."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:34
msgid "Plain JSON lines"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:36
msgid "The output is one JSON object per run, assembled by string concatenation, which keeps the runner free of serialization dependencies and easy to parse from Python."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:42
msgid "In diagnostic mode, the printed checksum equals the FNV-style fold of the per-call checksums, which the package's whitebox tests verify."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:44
msgid "Copies made for scratch cases never alias the prepared inputs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:48
msgid "**Timing each call separately.** Sub-microsecond calls are below the clock resolution; `repeat` amortizes it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:53
msgid "The runner measures one case per process, computes no statistics itself and supports only cases registered in `perf_support`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:1
msgid "perf_support design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:5
msgid "Benchmarks of numerical kernels are only useful if they are reproducible and comparable: the same inputs on every run and every machine, measurements of the computation rather than of setup, and a guarantee that the measured code actually ran. `perf_support` provides those guarantees for the benchmark subsystem of this repository."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:13
msgid "Deterministic inputs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:15
msgid ""
"Inputs are generated from a per-case seed with the SplitMix64 generator and mapped to a target distribution (for example uniform on $[-1, 1]$). Structured families are built from such draws by construction, for example a symmetric positive definite matrix as $M^{\\mathsf T} M + \\alpha I$, which is SPD for any $\\alpha > 0$ because $x^{\\mathsf T}(M^{\\mathsf T}M + \\alpha I)x = \\lVert Mx \\rVert^2 + \\alpha\\lVert x\\rVert^2 > 0$ for $x \\n"
"e 0$. The same seed always gives the same bits, so a fixture can be regenerated instead of stored."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:23
msgid "Checksums"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:25
msgid "Each run folds the bit patterns of its result into a 64-bit value with an FNV-style mix,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:33
msgid "where $w_k$ is the IEEE bit pattern of the $k$-th output value (shape first for matrices). The checksum serves two purposes: it keeps the compiler from removing the computation as dead code, and it detects a change of result bits between runs, targets or versions. It is not a cryptographic hash."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:40
msgid "Fixtures at runtime, registry in code"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:42
msgid "The case list is generated into MoonBit code (`generated_registry.mbt`) from `bench/datasets/manifest.json`, while the large input arrays live in JSON files that are loaded at runtime. This keeps the benchmark binary small, so that code size does not distort the measurements, and lets the Rust baseline consume the same fixtures."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:48
msgid "Self-healing fixtures"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:50
msgid "When a fixture file is missing, it is regenerated from the registry seed and written out, so a clean checkout can run any case. When a file exists but its version, metadata or shape disagrees with the registry, the run aborts: a silently mismatched input would make the numbers meaningless."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:55
msgid "Mutation policy"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:57
msgid "Some operations change their input (for example `reduce_row_elimination`). Cases with `mutation_policy = \"scratch_per_sample\"` therefore run on fresh copies, so every sample measures the same work."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:61
msgid "Unchecked kernels"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:63
msgid "The benchmarks call the `unchecked_*` methods, because the inputs are known to satisfy the preconditions and the cost of validation is not what is being measured."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:69
msgid "For a fixed dataset version, `prepare_case(c)` yields bit-identical inputs on every run."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:71
msgid "`run_prepared_case_inplace(p, true)` leaves `p` unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:72
msgid "The checksum depends on every output value and on the output shape."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:76
msgid "**Embedding all inputs in code.** Large literal arrays inflate the binary and compile time."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:78
msgid "**Random inputs per run.** Results would not be comparable across runs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:82
msgid "`perf_support` measures nothing itself; timing and statistics are in [`perf_runner`](perf_runner.md), [`perf`](perf.md) and `bench/run.py`. It covers only the `@mutable` package with `Double` inputs, and it is not part of the default test gate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:1
